{
 "script": "If both engines on your plane die, a tiny windmill is what keeps you flying.\n\nA jet with no engines doesn't fall like a rock. It glides — more than ten feet forward for every foot down. But no engines means no power. The screens go dark... and the controls steering this giant glider run on hydraulic pressure — that's draining away.\n\nThe yoke gets heavier. Here's what saves you.\n\nA hatch in the belly pops open, and a little propeller drops into the wind. It spins up, drives a pump, and pushes pressure back into the controls. Steering's back. A pinwheel out a car window — powered by your own speed.\n\nIn 1983, Air Canada 143 ran dry at forty-one thousand feet — someone mixed up pounds and kilograms. Luckily, the captain was a glider pilot. Unluckily, the runway they picked had a car race on it. He landed anyway. No serious injuries.\n\nAnd the plane? It kept flying for almost twenty-five more years.",
